<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # A Vessel Is Crossing! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting the Panama Canal</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Vessel Is Crossing!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Respond to a large variety of classroom commands and instructions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate, using simple language, a short story about a current event related to the Panama Canal (e.g., 'There are many people at the Canal today. They are watching the vessels pass by.').<br>Can respond to classroom commands and instructions about the Panama Canal to ask questions and clarify information (e.g., 'Point to the biggest vessel in the picture.' 'The red one?').<br>Can initiate and sustain conversations on familiar topics about the Canal in collaboration with peers (e.g., 'Which vessel is the biggest: the blue one or the white one?').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large, colorful picture of the Panama Canal with various vessels (ships, boats, yachts) crossing. The teacher uses TPR and commands: "Class, look at the picture! What do you observe?" "Point to a vessel!" "Is it big or small?" Teacher elicits short oral responses like "Vessel!", "Big!", "Ship!" to activate prior vocabulary related to 'canal,' 'vessel,' 'ship,' 'big,' 'small,' 'observe.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher states: "Today, we will talk about what we see at the Canal and compare things. Listen carefully to the words 'Canal' and 'boat'. Pay attention to the /k/ sound in 'Canal' and the /b/ sound in 'boat'. Repeat after me: 'Canal' /kænˈæl/, 'boat' /boʊt/, 'vessel' /ˈvɛsəl'." The teacher models simple sentences: "This vessel is big. That boat is small. They are crossing the canal."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to mouth: "Am I asking you to *write* or *speak* now?" (Expected answer: Speak!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher holds up a picture of a car and a ship: "Are we talking about cars or vessels at the Canal?" (Expected answer: Vessels at the Canal!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher gives a command: "Point to the *biggest* vessel in the picture." (Students point.) "The red one?" (Teacher confirms understanding.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated dialogue titled "At the Miraflores Locks!" between two 4th-grade children, Leo and Mia, visiting the Panama Canal. The dialogue features speech bubbles and images of different vessels, locks, and people.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Excerpt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "Look, Mia! A big vessel is crossing the canal now!" (<i>Present continuous, vocabulary: vessel, canal</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mia:</b> "Wow! It's so important! Is it bigger than the other ship?" (<i>Adjective: important, comparative: bigger, vocabulary: ship</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "Yes, it is! This vessel is the biggest one today. It connects the Atlantic and the Pacific Oceans." (<i>Superlative: biggest, verb: connects, noun: ocean</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mia:</b> "What goods is it transporting?" (<i>Present continuous interrogative, vocabulary: goods</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "I think it transports cars. Many people are observing it." (<i>Verb: transports, verb: observing</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mia:</b> "It's fascinating! Do you like the canal, Leo?" (<i>Adjective: fascinating, sociolinguistic: inviting participation</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "Yes, I like it because it is historic and wide." (<i>Adjectives: historic, wide</i>)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing current events at the Panama Canal, comparing vessels, and initiating and sustaining polite conversations about familiar topics related to the Canal.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue aloud, pointing to characters. Students listen and follow along, pointing to the vessels mentioned.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are Leo and Mia talking about?" (Expected answer: The Canal/Vessels)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The vessel is small. True or False?" (False) "Leo likes the Canal. True or False?" (True)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple (affirmative/interrogative), present continuous (affirmative/interrogative), comparatives and superlatives. Vocabulary: canal, vessel, ship, boat, yacht, goods, ocean, locks, observe, connect, transport, big, small, important, historic, fascinating. Pronunciation: /k/ in 'Canal', /b/ in 'boat'. Phonemic awareness: Spelling 'canal', 'vessel', blending /b/ /oʊt/ 'boat'. Pragmatic: describing current events, comparing things, describing facts. Sociolinguistic: polite exchanges.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Choral Repetition & Guided Questions):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher displays sentence frames from the dialogue and points to them. Students repeat chorally and individually: "A big vessel is crossing the canal now." "This vessel is bigger than that ship." "What goods is it transporting?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows flashcards of various vessels (e.g., a yacht, a ship, a small boat). Teacher asks: "Is this vessel *big* or *small*?" "Is this vessel *bigger* than the yacht?" Students respond with full sentences, e.g., "It is big." "Yes, it is bigger than the yacht."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-Controlled Substitution Drill (Picture-Based Comparisons):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a series of three pictures (e.g., a small boat, a medium ship, a large vessel) passing through the Canal locks.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides a sentence starter: "Vessel A is... Vessel B is..." In pairs, students take turns pointing to two vessels and asking: "Which vessel is bigger: the green one or the red one?" The partner answers using comparatives or superlatives: "The red vessel is bigger than the green one." or "The blue vessel is the biggest."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, listening for and correcting pronunciation of /k/ in 'canal' and /b/ in 'boat', and reinforcing correct grammar for comparatives.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction (Describing Current Events):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive different pictures showing various current events at the Canal (e.g., people watching from a bridge, a vessel transporting containers, a yacht entering the locks).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In pairs, students describe what they see in their picture using the present continuous: "Many people *are watching* the vessel pass by." "The vessel *is transporting* goods." They then ask their partner questions like: "What are the people doing?" or "What is the ship transporting?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Visiting the Panama Canal"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired role-play: "Our Canal Report"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher sets the scene: "Imagine you are a reporter and a visitor at the Panama Canal. You are talking live about a vessel that is crossing right now!" Students work in pairs, each receiving a simple role card with prompts.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reporter's Card:</b> "Hello, we are live at the Panama Canal! What do you observe today? What vessels are crossing? Which one is the biggest? What is happening now? Is the Canal important to you?" (Focus on initiating questions, present continuous for current events, comparatives/superlatives)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Visitor's Card:</b> "Oh, it's fascinating! Look, a big vessel *is crossing* right now. It *is bigger than* that yacht. Many people *are observing* it. I think the Canal *connects* two oceans. Yes, I like it because it is *historic*." (Focus on narrating, describing facts, comparing, using target vocabulary, polite responses)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students are encouraged to use present simple, present continuous, comparatives, superlatives, and at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., vessel, ship, canal, locks, observe, connect, important, big, small, goods). They should also practice respectful conversations by inviting their partner to participate ("What do you think?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Our Canal Report" role-play.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Our Canal Report" role-play for the teacher or selected classmates. Each pair has 1-2 minutes to present.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target sounds (/k/ in 'Canal', /b/ in 'boat') and words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 4 target vocabulary words related to the Canal (e.g., vessel, canal, observe, big, small, connect).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of Present Simple and Present Continuous to describe facts and current events.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of comparatives (e.g., 'bigger than') and/or superlatives (e.g., 'the biggest').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear communication and collaborative interaction with their partner (e.g., asking/answering questions, inviting participation).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete one of the following on a small paper slip before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I can talk about _________ and _________ at the Panama Canal."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new thing I learned to say today is: _________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you when talking about the Panama Canal today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and how can you practice more to speak better?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The students showed good engagement in describing vessels and current events at the Canal, especially using the present continuous. The structured pair activities provided sufficient scaffolding for using comparatives and superlatives. Some students still need more practice with forming interrogative sentences (e.g., "What are the ships transporting?"). Pronunciation of /k/ and /b/ improved, but continued reinforcement will be beneficial. I will ensure to provide more visual prompts for questioning in future activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 4 (Writing), students will use the oral descriptions and comparisons from today to construct simple sentences and short paragraphs about vessels crossing the Panama Canal, solidifying their understanding through written output.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>